#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171831 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "2a7b5d23-3dc7-57f8-98ed-72b1a45e787a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8157" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cba1591f419653a58de73519f06b9c64b57e92b88376a7a3a1a22f62e45bc74.svg)\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/db/dbe82125589c07f379dc34f725c5052ebf2f50ace729a5294d749bc82e5f796c.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7d2dc7ea-947d-5cda-8c95-c28281ecd792" :question/math-academy-id "e-8158" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff3e6c7ad9b4c8aa8e45f374a284c0f55a4655acab5ce77650e5e5eab8a3eea7.svg)\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D=P^T \\! A P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=5\\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/7e/7e2e5d56fa414820a4a21b54c1c382e822da079044416e35d511f75a8b4a4a3f.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](images/69/69886ad4b83344964ea9341595b201aaf8725d805afc0b71036413708ecbe1bf.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/4d/4d63a240ce9979a10db9b4b339c5e66387f030b7434e6fe544d32b1a122cb94d.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](images/f6/f604278ad5afd886a82142b3511ca042a7a1568c2dabb901ae7fb117508c2953.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/54/54e0d9e7d7cd07271dfc6a6c3439a7c394e5bf3d85f0630dd1c6f9889561f2c0.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "fd780d94-dd75-5a57-b34d-4bbaffcb95ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8164" :question/problem "![](images/71/71ae5f07963d6fc4006ac682fb99d9654957564b0238494aa5d6f2c6ab3a1161.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/c0/c037ac599b0f26311ef49cea15a50b3c215f5f66af6677cc400470e36ac1dc12.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/e8/e89397c2eb8ac9affa119178536b827fe4c9f710c5569dbdc5138c3d6498a413.svg)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/cb/cbbbb9019a6fe09c63e1a69478321553144988117e35d9947336c964841b9cfc.svg)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/ea/ea9a9bf2fc7a7328202006234fa0288237b663ae31c382cc0e98f301eb3999ae.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2512f3a3-20fb-505e-aa3a-5251056c13ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269596} {:answer/id #uuid "574019c5-ee82-51f0-8bf1-fc528162b812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270039} {:answer/id #uuid "59682479-9091-54d7-94ac-8fcf8d47bfb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270061} {:answer/id #uuid "77a78792-307d-52f7-b8e7-a3874f86b239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270329} {:answer/id #uuid "f5295862-adaf-5c67-9a31-fbdd0e3e18bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59682479-9091-54d7-94ac-8fcf8d47bfb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270061} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3714f5-a8f9-5d60-a0db-adf3ee87948c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ceb5619f-2165-5346-b048-85008466cdea" :question/math-academy-id "q-140780" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532bfb59-affa-5c62-a8e7-358e36a9bc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270008} {:answer/id #uuid "aea60c28-5e87-5191-952b-3b00f7cb6adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270821} {:answer/id #uuid "c179c9da-d404-5cbd-968a-2220b6fba57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270984} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3ecd7b-55a3-5ef3-ad46-ad378f716f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271197} {:answer/id #uuid "fc6dc910-3586-5cbb-930a-6f920df14dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea60c28-5e87-5191-952b-3b00f7cb6adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270821} :answer-field/id #uuid "34f87073-a16a-52a8-8a68-7c23a2ba2530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6cb0f9e-f73e-53e5-b7a2-f0d1aeb7047d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140825" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ea1678-0464-5847-b42f-729bf0e8d8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269735} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd1622e-6ab5-5f78-9167-ee025db03178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270247} {:answer/id #uuid "901f840a-a58d-5541-b4cc-78f9ec3f395b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270525} {:answer/id #uuid "be31c22b-ddcc-5f2d-95df-0a26ebb9e765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270948} {:answer/id #uuid "e036c742-0e1e-577c-b9ec-563f3052514f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32ea1678-0464-5847-b42f-729bf0e8d8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269735} :answer-field/id #uuid "a995ec0b-d934-5185-b9f2-a89cc802ee30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d04c9e9d-40ba-57d1-8646-0a16510ea6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140828" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db62ee4-2a6e-5aca-99a2-279b4cdde2da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491479} {:answer/id #uuid "1e147f75-020d-5d8c-b2c5-aeaaf906cd68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491516} {:answer/id #uuid "7ade7dd9-f5fe-54b0-9b8e-f4562b03352e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491668} {:answer/id #uuid "c53dca38-f58e-5432-8695-431c6b85b38e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491792} {:answer/id #uuid "c856dfd5-97c0-5fc2-9f6b-8bd13f9b8e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ade7dd9-f5fe-54b0-9b8e-f4562b03352e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491668} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cc9b8d0-ff97-509a-86fb-5808b2c08721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "338f7fda-0eb6-5e0f-ab2e-d2b60ab1cc4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93586" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085dfb60-f817-52bd-b510-171511c7be34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491471} {:answer/id #uuid "3a90f545-ef2d-5c81-b959-9c7ae8555686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491566} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5f2132-d567-563d-a029-6013020ee99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491569} {:answer/id #uuid "42bf69b6-