gonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1afbd007-f1e6-5e55-8cbe-4bcfee30c365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491507} {:answer/id #uuid "4a38eb59-71d6-57ae-b362-18321880abad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491595} {:answer/id #uuid "6b57765b-b3fc-58da-ae16-8958c804d7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491646} {:answer/id #uuid "6c66f93f-55cb-5fc9-800c-1394bd01e241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491649} {:answer/id #uuid "7873082b-15f9-5724-b785-35989be3f136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a38eb59-71d6-57ae-b362-18321880abad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491595} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4fd8bc-59fc-526b-b28d-cb3ea2256c56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6397dc6a-e670-5a27-9059-a00e14d9ffee" :question/math-academy-id "q-94024" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af7ba2c-f995-5cdb-b7ee-cabe8c1cbeac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491506} {:answer/id #uuid "3e19570d-c86d-5aef-8ce6-081272d7624d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491576} {:answer/id #uuid "b134fbf7-d5b6-5f41-ab04-2a7e561d26d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491763} {:answer/id #uuid "b82e7bd9-f08e-549b-a5ee-19697ab81504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491773} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c9344d-89ec-5291-9170-aea2d82d9332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b134fbf7-d5b6-5f41-ab04-2a7e561d26d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491763} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14357fc-2106-5858-9b65-b92159c22d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8da40f3d-5d18-5923-96f9-268309e6cbba" :question/math-academy-id "q-94029" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4661f467-2570-50d1-8ea7-764925bd6483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394896} {:answer/id #uuid "e507e2a2-00b8-50ad-bb5f-c430ff99f487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394897} {:answer/id #uuid "6b92abc9-a55a-50fc-ab02-ea6cb5653f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394898} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc656d3-5ba8-5298-bf61-43af779427a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394899} {:answer/id #uuid "13967d9f-3611-5bfe-bb71-32016afcadc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4661f467-2570-50d1-8ea7-764925bd6483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394896} :answer-field/id #uuid "7113dc5c-6527-51ab-8e9c-b39f83dfb5dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5923242-1a47-5487-a3a8-4c37dcb336a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94037" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ Computing $A-(-9)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+9I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "349a333f-7c5a-5481-82b0-60cb63d7e507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491558} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3fb012-e655-54c0-b54f-afddf4c20499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491644} {:answer/id #uuid "94417bd7-eb48-5c39-9406-4f41780ccd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491707} {:answer/id #uuid "c96bbaba-0f0d-5501-965f-1c3ab78bf9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491796} {:answer/id #uuid "fa236c72-29cd-50bc-b16a-514c5b7d0eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa236c72-29cd-50bc-b16a-514c5b7d0eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491880} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f366c1-707a-566c-ae94-1cc85de0d171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71c26421-0b8a-5150-b5ca-ed9ed8ecdd75" :question/math-academy-id "q-94038" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ Computing $A-(-6)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]+6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56f0e8db-19ca-5e1a-acef-433c78628572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364511} {:answer/id #uuid "1823a197-4084-5668-86ee-6f6631856c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364512} {:answer/id #uuid "f00dd1c5-3fcc-5dff-bacb-b531e82059c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364513} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d58908-b478-5a13-939f-a731e544060f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364514} {:answer/id #uuid "8cee7f48-d011-59dc-ac39-3664c1537063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1823a197-4084-5668-86ee-6f6631856c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364512} :answer-field/id #uuid "537c6748-dc18-5328-8608-350f3c43bf42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "987ecebc-d297-5962-b8d0-90f351ffca4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94043" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-10$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-10)I & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{aligned}]+10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-10)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56bc7cf9-dd1b-5316-add9-231be19d109f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364518} {:answer/id #uuid "b27a1670-2d5b-5bba-92a6-f0a17c0ce246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364519} {:answer/id #uuid "881bcb91-1f0d-5fd7-97a5-86876b601912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364520} {:answer/id #uuid "d84e1a54-ac78-5b2a-a104-f35ae3afdc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364521} {:answer/id #uuid "db75b88c-c492-572e-968f-86146a9098af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "881bcb91-1f0d-5fd7-97a5-86876b601912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364520} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd4411f-b5f0-53e2-9b19-b5941f836d1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7cf8708f-5209-58e9-a19a-fa6e74f6f1a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94044" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e29233-e0dc-59a9-8b71-89547fc06293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491564} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8be98c-dbfa-5fb0-9905-3ffacd2e675f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} & \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491601} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbc487c-5094-5d5f-8c80-f2cbf38f3c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491728} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cf2940-8c8f-50ee-bc73-4ae0c1be7870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491740} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb57498-4f30-5829-bbc1-a63f82ea49d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbb57498-4f30-5829-bbc1-a63f82ea49d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491826} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc785a6c-6e3c-5d7b-a2af-c97237d81ba3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23817e7f-6a06-51ca-bb89-ae0fdb8c7004" :question/math-academy-id "q-94049" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83034cba-0c66-5f4a-b7ab-09a5c1a7fcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404627} {:answer/id #uuid "febd8376-fe28-5c27-8963-ff102a571340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404628} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6af10c-5fa3-59e2-8df1-756aa2dce145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404629} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5de280-13f5-504f-9445-8833d1c988ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404630} {:answer/id #uuid "69324513-aecc-5af8-be4a-19ff3184bb08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83034cba-0c66-5f4a-b7ab-09a5c1a7fcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404627} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f41eb2e-7fb3-52bc-a716-8a62499f8e67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f738fa7a-a5ce-518d-9ada-841b441fb202" :question/math-academy-id "q-94056" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
